`channel`, a ceesafe_chan of u32 the\n * runtime takes a count of, as 1 (interrupt) or 2 (terminate), and dropped if the channel is full. 0, or a negated\n * errno: EEXIST while a subscription lasts, EOPNOTSUPP where there are no signals. Unsubscribing restores what the\n * signals did before; it names the subscription's channel, and does nothing for any other (there is no subscription,\n * or the value is a parent's, in a process made by fork(), which starts with none). Both may be called from any\n * thread. */\nint64_t ceesafe_signal_subscribe(void *channel);\nvoid ceesafe_signal_unsubscribe(void *channel);\n\n/* ---- Threads and mutexes (type-system.md §38; runtime/hosted/ceesafe_rt_thread.c), and atomics (here) ---------- */\n\ntypedef struct cee_thread cee_thread;\n/* Starts a thread running body(arg); NULL if it or its record could not be made. */\ncee_thread *ceesafe_thread_start(void *(*body)(void *), void *arg);\n/* Waits for the thread to end, then frees its record. */\nvoid ceesafe_thread_join(cee_thread *t);\n/* A platform mutex in allocated memory, or NULL. */\nvoid *ceesafe_mutex_create(void);\nvoid ceesafe_mutex_lock(void *m);\n/* True if the lock was free and is now held; never waits (ADR 0112). */\nbool ceesafe_mutex_try_lock(void *m);\nvoid ceesafe_mutex_unlock(void *m);\nvoid ceesafe_mutex_destroy(void *m);\n/* The lock a OnceLock's initialiser runs under (ADR 0096): one for the process, not allocated, and recursive, so an\n * initialiser may fill another OnceLock. */\nvoid ceesafe_mutex_once_lock(void);\nvoid ceesafe_mutex_once_unlock(void);\n/* ---- Atomics (§38.3): sequentially consistent, and with an ordering (ADR 0021); compare-exchange returns the\n * previous value. Static inline (ADR 0026), so an operation is its instructions where the compiler inlines it, and\n * a freestanding build needs no runtime for them. An ordering is lib/core's code: 0 relaxed, 1 acquire, 2 release, 3 acquire-release, 4 sequentially\n * consistent. lib/core passe